<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<chapter xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude" xml:id="ch-ble">
    <title>Bluetooth Low Energy</title>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            The goal of this chapter is to connect your final project to your phone:
            your Nucleo board will send information over a radio link and respond to
            commands coming back over the same link.  The radio protocol is
            <term>Bluetooth Low Energy</term> (BLE), and the radio itself is the
            Adafruit Bluefruit LE UART Friend, a small board that carries the entire
            BLE protocol on its own processor and connects to our microcontroller as
            a serial port, which you have used since the UART chapter.  Your
            program sends and receives bytes through USART1, the STM32C031C6's
            other USART, and the module carries those bytes over the air to and
            from an app on your phone.
        </p>
        <p>
            This chapter covers one class day.  Before class, the reading introduces
            BLE as a set of ideas: what the protocol is for, how two BLE devices find
            each other and exchange data, and how the Bluefruit module turns all of
            that into a serial connection.  In class we'll look at what it takes to
            talk to the module from your Nucleo, walk through the bring-up path your
            project will follow in Lab 9, and then give you time to work on the
            project.  <xref ref="sec-ble-reference"/> collects the flow-control
            mechanism, the AD2's Protocol tab, the AT commands and the module's
            timing rules for looking up while you build.
        </p>
        <objectives>
            <ul>
                <li>Explain what Bluetooth Low Energy is designed for, and how it differs from Bluetooth Classic, which is what streams audio to headphones and speakers.</li>
                <li>Describe how a BLE peripheral and a central find each other (the Generic Access Profile, GAP) and how they exchange data once connected (the Generic Attribute Profile, GATT: services and characteristics).</li>
                <li>Explain why we use a self-contained BLE module, and what it means that the Bluefruit presents a UART bridge.</li>
                <li>Identify the Bluefruit's pins and its two operating modes, and state which documents answer the wiring and configuration questions in Lab 9.</li>
                <li>Trace a character from CoolTerm through the passthrough program to the phone app, and back.</li>
            </ul>
        </objectives>
    </introduction>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <section xml:id="sec-ble-before-class">
        <title>Before Class: Bluetooth Low Energy</title>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                In the UART chapter we sent characters between your Nucleo and CoolTerm
                over a wire.  In the final project we'll send that same kind of data to
                your phone instead, over a radio link.  This reading covers what
                Bluetooth Low Energy is for, the three BLE communication modes, the
                roles the two devices play, how they find each other and exchange
                data, and how the Bluefruit module turns all of that into a serial
                connection.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-ble-what">
            <title>What BLE Is For</title>
            <p>
                The name Bluetooth covers two different protocols.  They use the same
                radio band, but they are designed for two different jobs.  The
                Bluetooth you know from headphones and speakers is now called
                <term>Bluetooth Classic</term>.  It was designed for continuous data
                streaming: audio, higher data rates, connections that stay up for
                hours.  Keeping a radio on that much costs power, which is fine for a
                speaker with a wall plug or a phone with a large battery.  Bluetooth
                Classic is built to stay connected and stream a lot.
            </p>
            <p>
                <term>Bluetooth Low Energy</term> (BLE) was designed for a different
                job: tiny bursts of data, sent occasionally, from devices that run on
                small batteries.  A fitness tracker sends a heart-rate number in
                short updates; a sensor node sends a temperature reading once a
                minute.  Between bursts the radio is off and the device draws very
                little current, which is how a coin cell can power such a device
                and last months.  A BLE
                device wakes up, sends a small amount of data, and goes back to sleep.
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-ble-classic-vs-ble">
                <caption>The two protocols and the jobs they are designed for.
                Left: headphones, which stream audio continuously over Bluetooth
                Classic.  Right: a battery-powered wearable, which sends a small
                reading now and then over Bluetooth Low Energy.</caption>
                <sidebyside widths="42% 42%" margins="4%" valign="middle">
                    <image source="images/Day17-BLE/slide06_img1.png"/>
                    <image source="images/Day17-BLE/slide06_img2.png"/>
                </sidebyside>
            </figure>
            <p>
                Our final project fits BLE's pattern exactly.  The system you build
                will send short status messages to your phone and receive short
                commands from it, a few bytes at a time.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-ble-modes">
            <title>BLE Connection Modes</title>
            <p>
                BLE supports three modes of communication
                (<xref ref="fig-ble-topologies"/>).  In <term>point-to-point</term>
                mode, two devices form a dedicated connection: a central and a
                peripheral, defined in the next subsection, exchange data in both
                directions in a structured way.  This is the most common BLE mode,
                and it is the one we will use.  In <term>broadcast</term> mode, one
                device transmits small packets to everyone nearby, one way, with no
                connection at all; some fitness sensors work this way.  In
                <term>mesh</term> mode, many devices relay messages from one to the
                next so that data can cross a large space, with no single device in
                charge; environmental monitoring in a greenhouse is a typical use.
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-ble-topologies">
                <caption>The three BLE communication modes, left to right:
                point-to-point between two devices, broadcast from one device to
                everyone nearby, and a mesh in which messages hop from device to
                device.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day17-BLE/fig-ble-topologies.png" width="90%"/>
            </figure>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-ble-roles">
            <title>Central and Peripheral</title>
            <p>
                In a point-to-point connection the two devices play fixed, different
                roles.  The <term>central</term> initiates the communication and
                requests data.  In our system the central is your phone.  In the
                usual arrangement, and in ours, the central is also called the
                <term>client</term>.
            </p>
            <p>
                The <term>peripheral</term> is the device that has the data.  In our
                system the peripheral is the Bluefruit module.  In BLE's vocabulary it
                is also called the <term>server</term>, because it stores the data,
                services, and characteristics that the client reads, writes, or
                subscribes to.  Services and characteristics have precise meanings
                in BLE, and we'll define them in <xref ref="subsec-ble-gatt"/>.
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-ble-central">
                <caption>A phone holding connections to several BLE peripherals at
                once.  Each of those connections is point-to-point, with one
                central and one peripheral, and the phone is the central in every
                one of them.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day17-BLE/fig-ble-central.png" width="60%"/>
            </figure>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-ble-gap">
            <title>How a Connection Is Made: GAP</title>
            <p>
                Before two devices can exchange data, they have to find each other.
                The rules for that are the <term>Generic Access Profile</term> (GAP),
                the part of Bluetooth that controls advertising and connections.  The
                peripheral <term>advertises</term>: it transmits a short packet, over
                and over, containing its name, the services it supports, and a unique
                identifier for each service, called a Universally Unique Identifier
                (UUID).  A UUID is a long number that identifies one service
                unambiguously, so that a phone scanning a room full of devices can
                tell what each device offers.  The central scans for advertisements and decides which
                device to connect to.  As soon as a connection is established, the
                peripheral stops advertising itself, and other devices will no longer
                be able to see it or connect to it until the existing connection is
                broken.
            </p>
            <p>
                You will see all of this on your own phone in Lab 9: the app's scan
                list is built from the advertising packets it receives, and once
                you connect to your module, your module stops advertising and drops
                out of other phones' scans.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-ble-gatt">
            <title>How Communication Happens: GATT</title>
            <p>
                Once a dedicated connection is established, data moves according to
                the <term>Generic Attribute Profile</term> (GATT), which defines how
                two BLE devices transfer data back and forth.  GATT arranges the
                peripheral's data in a small hierarchy.  A <term>service</term> is a
                category of functionality, and a peripheral can offer several.  Each
                service contains one or more <term>characteristics</term>, and a
                characteristic is one piece of data.  Each characteristic carries a
                <term>value</term>, which is the actual data, and
                <term>properties</term>, which are the rules for using it: whether
                the central may read it, write it, or subscribe to be notified when
                it changes.
            </p>
            <p>
                Here is a concrete example of that hierarchy.  Team 15's Bluefruit
                device offers one service, which the team named <em>ENGS 28 Super
                Cool Final Project</em>.  Inside it are two characteristics.  The
                first is a temperature, whose value could for example be 72 °F and
                whose property is read, so the central can request the current
                value at any time.  The second is a motor speed, whose value is
                for example 65 rpm and whose property is write, so the central can
                send a new value.  A third property, notify (not used in the
                example), lets the peripheral push a new value to the central
                whenever the value changes, without being asked.  Your own
                project's data does not appear as its own service; it rides inside
                the UART service's two characteristics
                (<xref ref="subsec-ble-bluefruit"/>).
            </p>
            <tabular halign="left">
                <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
                    <cell>The GATT layer</cell>
                    <cell>Team 15's instance</cell>
                </row>
                <row><cell>Device</cell><cell>Team 15's Bluefruit module</cell></row>
                <row><cell>Service, a category of functionality</cell><cell><em>ENGS 28 Super Cool Final Project</em></cell></row>
                <row><cell>Characteristic, one piece of data</cell><cell>Temperature: value 72 °F, property read</cell></row>
                <row><cell>Characteristic, another piece of data</cell><cell>Motor speed: value 65 rpm, property write</cell></row>
            </tabular>
            <p>
                A message board is a good analogy for the rhythm of the exchange.
                The peripheral wakes up and begins advertising, and its advertising
                packet names its available services; the characteristics inside
                them are discovered once the central connects.  Each characteristic
                defines its rules, and those rules define how the client can
                interact with it.  There is no constant conversation: the
                peripheral pins a note to a board and the central reads it once it
                has connected, rather than the two of them talking continuously.
                The peripheral sends a structured update when there is something to
                send and stays silent the rest of the time, and that is what makes
                BLE cheap enough in power to run from a coin cell.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-ble-bluefruit">
            <title>The Bluefruit Module: BLE as a UART</title>
            <p>
                A complete BLE implementation is a large piece of software: the
                radio's own firmware, the advertising and connection machinery of
                GAP, the attribute database of GATT, encryption, and the timing
                rules that tie them together.  We will not write any of that
                ourselves.  Instead we use the <term>Adafruit Bluefruit LE UART
                Friend</term> (<xref ref="fig-bluefruit-pinout"/>), a module that
                runs the whole BLE stack on its own processor.  The module connects
                to our microcontroller through a USART, and the service it offers
                over the air is a UART service, with a characteristic for each
                direction.  A phone that subscribes to the UART service's transmit
                characteristic receives every byte our program transmits, and every
                character typed on the phone arrives as a received byte in our
                program.  The outcome is
                that the Nucleo and the phone can talk to each other as if they had
                a direct USART connection.  The app on the phone side is Adafruit's
                Bluefruit LE Connect, and the link to install it is in Lab 9.
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-bluefruit-pinout">
                <caption>The Adafruit Bluefruit LE UART Friend.  The silver metal
                box is the radio module that runs the BLE stack.  The pads carry, by
                silkscreen name, DFU (device firmware update), GND (ground), VIN
                (the module's power supply, which we run at 3.3 V), RXI (UART
                receive, from the microcontroller into the module), TXO (UART
                transmit, from the module to the microcontroller), CTS (clear to
                send) and RTS (request to send), the two flow-control wires that
                carry each side's ready-or-not state, and MOD (mode selection).
                The switch labeled UART and CMD selects the module's mode, and the
                MOD pin makes the same selection under software control.  Image
                from the Adafruit guide.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day17-BLE/fig-bluefruit-pinout.png" width="100%"/>
            </figure>
            <p>
                The module has two operating modes, selected by the switch on the
                board, or by a Nucleo GPIO pin driving MOD.  In <term>data
                mode</term> (the switch position labeled UART) the module streams
                raw data: every byte that arrives on the board via RXI goes out
                over the air, and every byte the module receives from the air gets
                placed and sent out on TXO.  UART is the switch's own
                label; Adafruit's documentation calls the mode Data Mode.  In
                <term>command mode</term> (the switch position labeled CMD) the
                module instead interprets what it receives on RXI as configuration
                commands, each beginning with the letters AT; you will use a few of
                them in Lab 9 to reset the module and give your device its name.
                The module also has two LEDs, labeled CONN and MODE on the
                board; CONN indicates the connection state, which is useful
                for debugging.
            </p>
            <p>
                CTS (clear to send) is an input that gates the module's
                transmissions: it is pulled high on the module by default, and it
                must be brought low before the module will send data out of TXO.
                In our wiring, the Nucleo's USART drives it low through its own
                RTS (request to send) output whenever it is ready to receive, and
                the module's RTS output does the same for us in the other
                direction; a design that does not use flow control ties the
                module's CTS to ground instead.  We'll look in class at how the
                USART is configured to do this.  The module's own documentation is the
                <url href="https://learn.adafruit.com/introducing-the-adafruit-bluefruit-le-uart-friend">Adafruit
                guide</url>, linked in Lab 9; its pinout page is the one to read
                before class.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <reading-questions xml:id="rq-ble">
            <exercise xml:id="rq-ble-pattern">
                <statement>
                    <p>Which of these applications matches what BLE was designed
                    for?</p>
                </statement>
                <choices randomize="yes">
                    <choice correct="yes">
                        <statement><p>A battery-powered sensor that sends a temperature reading once a minute.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>Correct.  Tiny bursts of data from a
                        battery-powered device are BLE's pattern: the device wakes
                        up, sends a small amount of data, and goes back to
                        sleep.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>Wireless headphones playing music.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>Audio is a continuous stream at a high data
                        rate, which is Bluetooth Classic's job.  BLE trades that
                        bandwidth away for low power consumption.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>Transferring a large video file between two laptops.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>A large transfer needs a fast, continuous
                        connection.  BLE sends small packets occasionally, and it
                        would be very slow at this.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>A wireless security camera streaming live video.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>Video streaming is continuous, high-rate
                        traffic.  BLE is built for short bursts of a few bytes, not
                        for streams.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                </choices>
            </exercise>

            <exercise xml:id="rq-ble-roles">
                <statement>
                    <p>In the final project, your phone connects to your team's
                    Bluefruit module.  Which device is the peripheral, and what does
                    the peripheral do before the connection exists?</p>
                </statement>
                <choices randomize="yes">
                    <choice correct="yes">
                        <statement><p>The Bluefruit module is the peripheral, and it advertises: it repeatedly transmits a short packet with its name and services until a central connects.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>Correct.  The peripheral advertises and the
                        central scans.  The phone, as the central, picks the module
                        out of its scan list and initiates the connection.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>The phone is the peripheral, because it is the smaller and more portable device.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>The roles are about who initiates and who
                        advertises, not about size.  The phone scans and initiates,
                        which makes it the central.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>The Bluefruit module is the peripheral, and before the connection it stays silent to save power until the phone finds it.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>A device that never transmits cannot be
                        discovered.  The peripheral advertises, and that is how the
                        central's scan finds it.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>Whichever device powers on first becomes the peripheral.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>The roles are fixed by design, not by timing.
                        The module is built to advertise and serve data; the phone
                        is built to scan and connect.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                </choices>
            </exercise>

            <exercise xml:id="rq-ble-advertising">
                <statement>
                    <p>Your module is connected to your phone.  A teammate opens the
                    app on their own phone and scans.  What do they see?</p>
                </statement>
                <choices randomize="yes">
                    <choice correct="yes">
                        <statement><p>Your module does not appear in their scan list, because a connected peripheral stops advertising until the connection is broken.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>Correct.  As soon as a connection is
                        established the peripheral stops advertising itself, and
                        other devices can no longer see it or connect to it.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>Your module appears, and connecting to it from the second phone joins a three-way conversation.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>A point-to-point connection has exactly one
                        central and one peripheral.  While your phone holds the
                        connection, the module is invisible to other
                        centrals.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>Your module appears, but connecting to it disconnects your phone.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>A connected peripheral is not advertising, so
                        it does not appear in the second phone's scan at all, and
                        there is nothing there to connect to.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                </choices>
            </exercise>

            <exercise xml:id="rq-ble-gatt">
                <statement>
                    <p>In GATT's hierarchy, what is a characteristic?</p>
                </statement>
                <choices randomize="yes">
                    <choice correct="yes">
                        <statement><p>One piece of data inside a service, with a value and properties saying whether it can be read, written, or subscribed to.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>Correct.  A service is a category of
                        functionality; each characteristic inside it is one piece
                        of data, and its properties are the rules for using
                        it.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>The name a peripheral broadcasts while advertising.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>The advertised name is part of GAP, the
                        discovery phase.  Characteristics belong to GATT, the data
                        exchange that starts after the connection is made.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>The radio channel a connection uses.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>Radio channels are far below GATT, in the part
                        of the protocol the module handles for us.  A characteristic
                        is a piece of data with rules attached, and it describes
                        nothing about the radio.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>A category of functionality that contains one or more services.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>That is the hierarchy upside down: a service
                        is the category, and it contains one or more
                        characteristics.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                </choices>
            </exercise>

            <exercise xml:id="rq-ble-bridge">
                <statement>
                    <p>Your program sends the characters <c>T=23</c> out of the USART
                    connected to the Bluefruit, which is in data mode and connected
                    to your phone.  What happens?</p>
                </statement>
                <choices randomize="yes">
                    <choice correct="yes">
                        <statement><p>The module forwards the bytes over the air, and <c>T=23</c> appears in the app's UART console on the phone.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>Correct.  In data mode the module is a bridge:
                        bytes in on RXI go out over the air through the UART
                        service, and the app displays them.  The Nucleo and the
                        phone talk as if they had a direct USART
                        connection.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>The module interprets the characters as a configuration command and responds OK or ERROR.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>Commands are interpreted only in command mode
                        (CMD).  In data mode every byte is forwarded as data,
                        whatever its value.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>The characters appear in CoolTerm on your laptop.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>CoolTerm is connected to a different USART,
                        the one that goes through the ST-LINK to your laptop.  The
                        bytes sent to the Bluefruit's USART go to the phone.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>Nothing, until the phone requests the data with a read.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>The UART service uses the notify property for
                        this direction: the module pushes the bytes to the phone as
                        they arrive, without being asked.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                </choices>
            </exercise>
        </reading-questions>
    </section>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <section xml:id="sec-ble-day17">
        <title>Day 17 In-Class: Bluetooth Low Energy, then Project Work Time</title>
        <!-- 7 min before Part 1: title, groupings (reseating to assigned team
             tables), where we are, agenda.  7 + 8 + 14 + 16 + 7 + 57 + 1 = 110. -->
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Today we'll look at what it takes to talk to the Bluefruit module
                from your Nucleo: the USART it uses, the flow-control wires it
                needs, the passthrough program that tests your driver, and the
                module's two modes.  Then we'll give you the rest of the class to
                work on your final project, which is Lab 9.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <!-- Part 1: 12 min = 2 (the module recall) + 5 (her slide 13 with the fill-in table) + 3 (flow control) + 2 (complete the table) -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day17-usart1">
            <title>Part 1: Using USART1 and Hardware Flow Control</title>
            <p>
                In the reading we introduced the Bluefruit module as a UART bridge.
                On our board, the bridge runs through <term>USART1</term>: the
                STM32C031C6 has two USARTs, and USART2 is already carrying
                <c>printf</c> to CoolTerm through the ST-LINK, so we'll use USART1
                to connect the Bluefruit module to the Nucleo.  We'll also be
                using hardware-based flow control.
            </p>
            <p>
                <term>Hardware flow control</term> is two extra wires that carry
                each side's ready-or-not state, so that neither side sends data
                faster than the other can take it in.  CTS (clear to send) is the
                input that allows a device to transmit: the
                <url href="external/stm32c031_rm.pdf">Reference Manual</url>
                (RM0490 §24.5.20) says that with flow control enabled, the
                transmitter checks its CTS input before sending each frame, and
                while that input is high it waits; the frame goes out once CTS is
                low again.  RTS (request
                to send) is the same signal from the other side's point of view: a
                device holds its RTS output low while it is ready to receive, and
                raises it when it is not.  The two wires cross: the Nucleo's RTS output
                drives the Bluefruit's CTS input, and the Bluefruit's RTS output
                drives the Nucleo's CTS input.  On the Bluefruit there is
                nothing to enable: its CTS gate is always in effect, and the
                module ships with that pin pulled high, so a module whose CTS is
                not wired will not transmit.  On our side the gate exists only
                once flow control is enabled: a USART with flow
                control enabled and its CTS input accidentally left unconnected
                can stop transmitting entirely, with no error appearing
                anywhere.
            </p>
            <p>
                Read the
                details of USART1 in the Reference Manual (RM0490 §24.5.20) to
                understand how to enable this feature.  Once you understand how to
                enable it, examine the alternate
                function mapping tables in the
                <url href="external/stm32c031_datasheet.pdf">STM32C031
                datasheet</url> (Tables 13 through 18, <em>Port x alternate
                function mapping</em>), which list which pins can carry each of the
                four USART1 signals.  You will need to decide which physical pins
                you will use for the four USART1 signals.
            </p>
            <p>
                The module's pins are the ones you met in the reading
                (<xref ref="fig-bluefruit-pinout"/>), and the
                four that matter here are RXI, TXO, CTS and RTS.
            </p>
            <activity xml:id="act-day17-af-table">
                <title>Complete your table: the Bluefruit connections</title>
                <statement>
                    <p>Fill in the table below: for each of the four USART1
                    signals, the port and pin you chose, its alternate function
                    number, and the pin on the Bluefruit you'll connect it
                    to.</p>
                    <tabular halign="left" top="minor" left="minor" bottom="minor" right="minor">
                        <col right="minor"/>
                        <col right="minor"/>
                        <col right="minor"/>
                        <col right="minor"/>
                        <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
                            <cell>USART function</cell>
                            <cell>Port/Pin</cell>
                            <cell>Alt Func</cell>
                            <cell>Bluefruit pin</cell>
                        </row>
                        <row bottom="minor"><cell>USART1 TX</cell><cell>&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;</cell><cell>&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;</cell><cell>RXI</cell></row>
                        <row bottom="minor"><cell>USART1 RX</cell><cell>&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;</cell><cell>&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;</cell><cell>&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;</cell></row>
                        <row bottom="minor"><cell>USART1 CTS</cell><cell>&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;</cell><cell>&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;</cell><cell>&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;</cell></row>
                        <row bottom="minor"><cell>USART1 RTS</cell><cell>&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;</cell><cell>&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;</cell><cell>CTS</cell></row>
                    </tabular>
                </statement>
            </activity>
            <slide xml:id="sl-day17-bridge">
    <sidebyside widths="55% 41%" margins="0%" valign="middle">
        <stack>
            <ul>
                <li>The module connects to your Nucleo via a USART and runs the whole BLE stack on its own processor.</li>
                <li>Two operating modes, selected by the little switch: in command mode (CMD) the module listens for AT commands, and in data mode (UART) it streams raw data.</li>
                <li>The pads we use today: VIN, GND, RXI, TXO, CTS, RTS and MOD.</li>
                <li>The built-in LED is useful for debugging the connection state.</li>
            </ul>
        </stack>
        <stack>
            <image source="images/Day17-BLE/fig-bluefruit-pinout.png" width="100%"/>
        </stack>
    </sidebyside>
    <note>≈ 2 min.  Her slide 12's bullets beside the reading's pinout figure; a recall, not a re-teaching.</note>
</slide>
            <slide xml:id="sl-day17-usart1">
    <ul>
        <li>The Bluefruit connects to your Nucleo via a USART.  USART2 is already carrying <c>printf</c> to CoolTerm through the ST-LINK, so we'll use USART1.</li>
        <li>We'll also be using hardware-based flow control: read the details of USART1 in the Reference Manual (RM0490 §24.5.20) to understand how to enable this feature.</li>
        <li>Once you understand how to enable it, examine the alternate function mappings in the STM32C031 datasheet (Tables 13 through 18).  You will need to decide which physical pins you will use for the four USART1 signals.</li>
        <li>Fill in the table below with the pins you intend to use: for each of the four USART1 signals, the port and pin, and its alternate function number.</li>
    </ul>
    <sidebyside widths="46%" margins="27%">
    <tabular halign="left" top="minor" left="minor" bottom="minor" right="minor">
        <col right="minor"/>
        <col right="minor"/>
        <col right="minor"/>
        <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
            <cell>USART function</cell>
            <cell>Port/Pin</cell>
            <cell>Alt Func</cell>
        </row>
        <row bottom="minor"><cell>USART1 TX</cell><cell>&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;</cell><cell>&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;</cell></row>
        <row bottom="minor"><cell>USART1 RX</cell><cell>&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;</cell><cell>&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;</cell></row>
        <row bottom="minor"><cell>USART1 CTS</cell><cell>&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;</cell><cell>&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;</cell></row>
        <row bottom="minor"><cell>USART1 RTS</cell><cell>&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;</cell><cell>&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;</cell></row>
    </tabular>
    </sidebyside>
    <note>≈ 5 min.  Her slide 13 with the fill-in table on the same slide (her ask, 2026-09-11): the documents stay readable while teams fill it in.  The lookups are theirs: name the documents, do not do them.</note>
</slide>
            <slide xml:id="sl-day17-flow">
    <ul>
        <li><term>Hardware flow control</term> is two extra wires that carry each side's ready-or-not state, so that neither side sends data faster than the other can take it in.</li>
        <li>CTS (clear to send) is the input that allows a device to transmit: with flow control enabled, the transmitter checks its CTS input before sending each frame, and while the input is high it waits.  The frame goes out once CTS is low again.</li>
        <li>RTS (request to send) is the same signal from the other side's point of view: a device holds its RTS output low while it is ready to receive, and raises it when it is not.</li>
        <li>The two wires cross: the Nucleo's RTS output drives the Bluefruit's CTS input, and the Bluefruit's RTS output drives the Nucleo's CTS input.</li>
        <li>A USART with flow control enabled and its CTS input accidentally left unconnected can stop transmitting entirely, with no error appearing anywhere.</li>
    </ul>
    <note>≈ 3 min.  Part 1's mechanism; the RM citation is in the book paragraph.</note>
</slide>
    <instructor xml:id="inst-day17-af-options">
            <p>The USART1 signal options from the datasheet's AF tables (Tables 13
            through 18), for answering teams working on Deliverable 1.  The choice
            is theirs; this table is not projected to students.</p>
            <ul>
                <li>USART1_TX: PA0 (AF4), PA9 (AF1), PB6 (AF0), PC14 (AF0).</li>
                <li>USART1_RX: PA1 (AF4), PA8 (AF14), PA10 (AF1), PB2 (AF0), PB7 (AF0).</li>
                <li>USART1_CTS: PA11 (AF1), PB4 (AF4), PB6 (AF5).</li>
                <li>USART1_RTS: PA12 (AF1), PA14 (AF12, but PA14 is also SWCLK/BOOT0, and a team that uses it loses SWD flashing and debugging), PA15 (AF4), PB3 (AF4), PB6 (AF4).  The datasheet spells this signal USART1_RTS_DE_CK in the AF tables.</li>
            </ul>
            <p>Conflicts to steer teams around, depending on their project: PB6
            and PB7 are D1 and D0, and are the Nucleo's default USART1 pins
            (UM2953 Table 11), which is the most relevant fact for Deliverable 1;
            PA2 and PA3 are USART2 to the ST-LINK; PA5 is the user LED; PC13 is
            the user button; PB4 is D5, the course's external button; PA15 is D7,
            the Day 12 photointerrupter; PA11 and PA12 carry CTS and RTS at AF1
            and are A4 and A5 on CN8 by default (solder bridges SB8/SB9), so a
            team using them gives up those analog inputs.  For a team whose
            transmit works with flow control disabled and stops when CTSE is
            enabled, the first thing to check is an unwired or miswired CTS: the
            module's RTS must reach the USART1 CTS pin they chose, and both
            flow-control wires cross rather than run straight.</p>
        </instructor>
        </subsection>

        <!-- Part 2: 18 min = 4 (driver brief) + 2 (where uart2_read blocks) + 3 (passthrough in words) + 2 (AD2 Protocol tab) + 4 (test brief) + 3 (reveal) -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day17-driver">
            <title>Part 2: Your USART1 Driver, and the Passthrough Test</title>
            <p>
                Lab 9 asks for your own driver for USART1, and we'll start
                writing it now.  You can
                mimic the USART2 driver we gave you in the UART
                chapter.  The one piece with no model in that driver is the
                availability check: <c>uart2_read()</c> waits until a byte
                arrives, which is not an appropriate way to handle incoming data
                in this project.  Your program will be running normally even in
                the absence of input arriving from your phone, and will react to
                input if any arrives, so you'll need a way to ask whether a byte
                has arrived without waiting for one.  You made this kind of non-blocking
                check once before, in the UART chapter's keyboard-counter
                exercise, where you polled the RXNE (receive data register not
                empty) flag yourself instead of
                calling <c>uart2_read()</c>.
            </p>
            <activity xml:id="act-day17-driver">
                <title>Write your USART1 driver</title>
                <task xml:id="task-day17-init">
                    <statement><p>Write <c>uart1_init()</c>.  You can start with
                    <c>uart2_init()</c> and modify it to use the pins that you
                    decided on previously.  Also remember that you need to enable
                    hardware flow control.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day17-rest">
                    <statement><p>Write the rest of your USART1 driver: mimic the
                    USART2 driver!</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day17-rxavail">
                    <statement><p>In the final system, you won't know which USART
                    will transmit first, so you must devise a way to determine
                    whether there are any characters in the receive data register.
                    Do not use the existing read function unless you already know
                    there is a byte waiting; otherwise, you may block
                    indefinitely.</p></statement>
                </task>
            </activity>
            <slide xml:id="sl-day17-uart2-read">
    <p><c>uart2_read()</c> from <c>uart.c</c> waits in its <c>while</c> loop on the RXNE flag (receive data register not empty) until a byte arrives.  If nothing ever arrives, it waits forever, and your program never gets to check the other USART.</p>
    <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
/* UART2 read function */
int uart2_read() {
    /* Make sure the receive data register is non-empty */
    while(!(USART2->ISR & USART_ISR_RXNE_RXFNE)){}   // <-- blocks here until a byte arrives
    /* Return data register */
    return(USART2->RDR & 0xFF);
}
]]></code></program>
    <note>≈ 2 min.  Her ask (2026-09-11): show uart2_read() and highlight where the blocking occurs.  Given driver code (uart.c), not a solution.</note>
</slide>
            <p>
                To test your driver before the Bluefruit is ever wired, you'll
                write a small program which connects the
                transmitter of USART2 (CoolTerm) to the receiver of USART1, and
                vice versa: whatever arrives on one USART is transmitted out the
                other.  Using the two UARTs in this manner is known as
                <term>passthrough</term>.  If characters cross in both
                directions, your transmit, your receive and your availability
                checks work.  The
                program is short: initialize both
                drivers, and in the main loop use your two availability checks so
                that neither direction ever blocks the other.
            </p>
            <slide xml:id="sl-day17-passthrough-intro">
    <ul>
        <li>To test your USART1 driver, write a small program which connects the transmitter of USART2 (CoolTerm) to the receiver of USART1, and vice versa.  Using the two UARTs in this manner is known as <term>passthrough</term>.</li>
        <li>The program is short.  Initialize both drivers.  Then, in an endless loop: if a character is available on USART1, read it and write it to USART2; if a character is available on USART2, read it and write it to USART1.  Your availability checks keep either read from blocking the other.</li>
        <li>If characters cross in both directions, your transmit, your receive and your availability checks all work.  A problem that appears after the Bluefruit is connected is then in the wiring or the module, not in your driver.</li>
        <li>Nothing is connected to USART1 yet; we'll use the AD2 as a device that can temporarily fill this role.</li>
    </ul>
    <note>≈ 3 min.  Her slide 15's description with more build guidance (her ask, 2026-09-11), still no code — the worked version is the instructor reveal.</note>
</slide>
            <p>
                Nothing is connected to USART1 yet, so we'll use the AD2 as a
                device that can temporarily fill this role.  The
                <term>Protocol</term> tab in the main Waveforms menu is new to
                us: in it the AD2 acts as another UART device, one that can both
                send characters into USART1's receiver and show what USART1
                transmits.  The AD2 does not support hardware flow control, so
                the passthrough test does not exercise the flow-control wires;
                those are first used once the Bluefruit module is connected.
            </p>
            <slide xml:id="sl-day17-ad2-protocol">
    <ul>
        <li>You can use your AD2 to observe the pins on USART1, and to send characters to it, confirming that it is working properly: use the <term>Protocol</term> tab in the main Waveforms menu (on the left-hand side of the welcome tab).</li>
        <li>The AD2 acts as another UART device that can send and receive.  In the protocol settings you define which AD2 DIO pin acts as Tx and which acts as Rx.</li>
        <li>Sending from CoolTerm: CoolTerm sends to USART2's receiver, your passthrough program passes the character on to USART1's transmitter, and the AD2 reads it on its Rx DIO pin.</li>
        <li>Sending from the AD2: the character arrives at USART1's receiver, your program passes it on to USART2's transmitter, and you will see it in CoolTerm.</li>
        <li>You need to be a little careful and send one character at a time, because the AD2 does not support hardware flow control.</li>
    </ul>
    <note>≈ 2 min.  Her slide 15's AD2 half: the Protocol tab is new today, introduced by name before the test activity asks for it.</note>
</slide>
            <activity xml:id="act-day17-passthrough-test">
                <title>Test your USART1 via passthrough</title>
                <task xml:id="task-day17-build-passthrough">
                    <statement><p>Write the passthrough program.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day17-ad2-wire">
                    <statement><p>Decide which DIO wire will act as the AD2's Tx
                    and which as its Rx.  Wire your Tx choice to your USART1
                    receive pin and your Rx choice to your USART1 transmit pin,
                    then set the Protocol tab's Tx and Rx to those same DIO
                    numbers, with the baud rate at 9600 to match your
                    driver.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day17-coolterm-to-ad2">
                    <statement><p>Type into CoolTerm.  Each character should
                    make the full trip: CoolTerm sends it to USART2's receiver,
                    your passthrough program passes it on to USART1's
                    transmitter, and the AD2 reads it on its Rx DIO pin and
                    shows it.  If a character does not show up on the AD2,
                    either something is incorrectly wired or set in Waveforms,
                    or the passthrough program is not quite right
                    yet.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day17-ad2-to-coolterm">
                    <statement><p>Send characters from the AD2, one at a time
                    (the AD2 does not support hardware flow control).  Each
                    character should make the trip in reverse: the AD2 sends it
                    from its Tx DIO pin to USART1's receiver, your program
                    passes it on to USART2's transmitter, and it appears in
                    CoolTerm.</p></statement>
                </task>
            </activity>
            <instructor xml:id="inst-day17-passthrough">
                <p>The worked passthrough, with the availability check for USART2
                beside it, for the reveal after teams have built their own.  This
                is her slide 16's code; the students' Deliverable 3 includes both
                availability checks, which is why neither is in the student
                book.</p>
                <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
/* passthrough.c */

#include <stdio.h>
#include "uart.h"
#include "uart1.h"
#include "ES28.h"

int uart2_RxAvail(void);

int main(void) {
    uart1_init();
    uart2_init();
    printf("UART1-2 passthrough test.\r\n");
    while (1) {
        if (uart1_RxAvail()) {
            int c = uart1_read();
            uart2_write(c);
        }
        if (uart2_RxAvail()) {
            int c = uart2_read();
            uart1_write(c);
        }
    }
    return 1;
}
]]></code></program>
                <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
int uart2_RxAvail(void) {
    if (USART2->ISR & USART_ISR_RXNE_RXFNE) {
        return 1;
    } else {
        return 0;
    }
}
]]></code></program>
            </instructor>
        </subsection>

        <!-- Part 3: 16 min = 4 (wiring + app) + 3 (UART mode) + 3 (CMD mode) + 5 (MOD pin) + 1 (ready) -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day17-connect">
            <title>Part 3: Connecting the Bluefruit, and Its Two Modes</title>
            <p>
                With the driver tested, we'll take the AD2 off USART1 and put the
                Bluefruit module there.  The connections are given in Lab 9 §4.2,
                and <xref ref="table-day17-wiring"/> collects them with their
                directions.
            </p>
            <table xml:id="table-day17-wiring">
                <title>The six connections, and their directions</title>
                <tabular halign="left">
                <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
                    <cell>Bluefruit pin</cell>
                    <cell>Direction</cell>
                    <cell>On your Nucleo</cell>
                </row>
                <row><cell>RXI</cell><cell>into the Bluefruit module</cell><cell>USART1 TX</cell></row>
                <row><cell>TXO</cell><cell>out of the Bluefruit module</cell><cell>USART1 RX</cell></row>
                <row><cell>RTS</cell><cell>out of the Bluefruit module</cell><cell>USART1 CTS</cell></row>
                <row><cell>CTS</cell><cell>into the Bluefruit module</cell><cell>USART1 RTS</cell></row>
                <row><cell>VIN</cell><cell>power</cell><cell>3.3 V</cell></row>
                <row><cell>GND</cell><cell>power</cell><cell>GND</cell></row>
                </tabular>
            </table>
            <activity xml:id="act-day17-connect">
                <title>Connect your Bluefruit to your Nucleo</title>
                <task xml:id="task-day17-wire">
                    <statement><p>With nothing powered, make the six connections in
                    the table above, using the Nucleo pins you picked earlier.  Note the
                    crossover in both pairs: your USART1 TX goes to the
                    Bluefruit's RXI, and the Bluefruit's TXO goes to your USART1
                    RX; your USART1 RTS goes to the Bluefruit's CTS, and the
                    Bluefruit's RTS goes to your USART1 CTS.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day17-app">
                    <statement><p>Install the Adafruit Bluefruit LE Connect app on
                    your phone.  The link is in Lab 9.</p></statement>
                </task>
            </activity>
            <figure xml:id="fig-bluefruit-switch">
                <caption>The mode switch on the Bluefruit board, circled.  Slide
                it toward UART for data mode and toward CMD for command mode; the
                two labels are printed beside it on the board.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day17-BLE/fig-bluefruit-switch.svg" width="35%"/>
            </figure>
            <p>
                The switch's own label for data mode is UART
                (<xref ref="fig-bluefruit-switch"/>), and Adafruit's
                documentation calls it data mode.  With the
                passthrough program running and the switch at UART, the app's
                UART tab and CoolTerm are two ends of one serial link.
            </p>
            <activity xml:id="act-day17-uart-mode">
                <title>Test UART mode (aka Data Mode in Adafruit speak)</title>
                <task xml:id="task-day17-switch-uart">
                    <statement><p>Slide the little switch on the Bluefruit board to
                    UART, and run the passthrough program on your
                    Nucleo.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day17-find">
                    <statement><p>Open the Adafruit Bluetooth app on your phone and
                    select the <q>Must have UART Service</q> filter.  Find and
                    connect to your device, and open the UART tab.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day17-both-ways">
                    <statement><p>Test sending and receiving characters over
                    Bluetooth.  Make sure that characters you type on your phone
                    appear in CoolTerm, and vice versa.</p></statement>
                </task>
            </activity>
            <slide xml:id="sl-day17-uart-mode" room="yes">
    <sidebyside widths="58% 38%" margins="0%" valign="middle">
        <stack>
            <ol>
                <li>Slide the little switch on the Bluefruit board to UART (circled at the right), and run the passthrough program on your Nucleo.</li>
                <li>Open the Adafruit Bluetooth app on your phone and select the <q>Must have UART Service</q> filter.  Find and connect to your device, and open the UART tab.</li>
                <li>Test sending and receiving characters over Bluetooth.  Make sure that characters you type on your phone appear in CoolTerm, and vice versa.</li>
            </ol>
        </stack>
        <stack>
            <image source="images/Day17-BLE/fig-bluefruit-switch.svg" width="100%"/>
        </stack>
    </sidebyside>
    <caption>Trouble finding your device?  Sort the app's list by signal strength, or give your device a name in command mode.  Connected, but no characters cross?  Check the crossover: the Bluefruit's RTS must reach the USART1 CTS pin you chose, and your RTS must reach the Bluefruit's CTS.</caption>
    <note>≈ 3 min.  Her slide 19: the steps with her circled-switch picture beside them.  The activity in the book carries the same steps.</note>
</slide>
            <p>
                If the app connects but no characters cross in either direction,
                check the crossover first: the Bluefruit's RTS must reach the
                USART1 CTS pin you chose, and your RTS must reach the
                Bluefruit's CTS.  If you have trouble finding your device, sort
                the app's list by signal strength, go to a space with fewer
                ENGS 28 students trying to find their devices, or give your
                device a name, which you do in command mode.
            </p>
            <p>
                Command mode is where the module is configured.  Instructions to
                the module come in the form of <term>AT commands</term>: short
                text lines, each starting with <c>AT</c>, that configure it or
                ask it to do something.  The
                name you assign with <c>AT+GAPDEVNAME=chosenName</c>
                is the name your phone's scan shows.
            </p>
            <activity xml:id="act-day17-cmd-mode">
                <title>CMD (command) mode</title>
                <task xml:id="task-day17-switch-cmd">
                    <statement><p>Place your Bluefruit into CMD mode by sliding
                    the little switch toward CMD, and keep the passthrough
                    program running on your Nucleo.  The module's responses (OK
                    or ERROR) will appear in CoolTerm.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day17-at">
                    <statement><p>Type a few AT commands into CoolTerm:</p>
                    <p><ul>
                        <li><c>ATZ</c> is a soft reset: it restarts the Bluetooth stack.</li>
                        <li><c>AT+FACTORYRESET</c> is the big hammer: it restores the module to a known state.</li>
                        <li><c>AT+GAPDEVNAME=chosenName</c> gives your device a recognizable name.  Issue an <c>ATZ</c> afterwards so that the device starts advertising the change.</li>
                    </ul></p>
                    <p>The command formats are described in the
                    <url href="https://learn.adafruit.com/introducing-the-adafruit-bluefruit-le-uart-friend/command-mode-1">Adafruit
                    documentation</url>, and the commands you need are given in
                    Lab 9 §4.3.</p></statement>
                </task>
            </activity>
            <p>
                You wouldn't want to keep sliding that little switch by hand, and
                you don't have to: the MOD pin is another way to select the mode
                without touching the switch.  Lab 9 points
                you to the Bluefruit schematic, where you can see how the two
                are wired together.
            </p>
            <activity xml:id="act-day17-mode-pin">
                <title>Switching modes via the MOD pin</title>
                <task xml:id="task-day17-gpio">
                    <statement><p>Connect a GPIO pin on your Nucleo to the MOD pin
                    on the Bluefruit.  You can control the mode by switching the
                    pin high (CMD mode) or low (UART mode).  In your code you'll
                    need to initialize the GPIO pin (as what kind of
                    pin?).</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day17-startup">
                    <statement><p>Make the BLE startup a routine of its own:</p>
                    <p><ul>
                        <li>switch the mode pin to CMD mode,</li>
                        <li>perform a factory reset,</li>
                        <li>assign a device name,</li>
                        <li>perform a soft reset (so the device will start advertising the new name),</li>
                        <li>and switch to UART mode.</li>
                    </ul></p>
                    <p>The device should then wait, doing nothing, until data
                    arrives.  Consider placing the initialization in its own file
                    as a function you can call from any program.</p></statement>
                </task>
            </activity>
            <p>
                You are then ready to build your system.  Your system should behave
                normally even if there is no other device connected.  When a
                Bluetooth device connects, it should start receiving data from your
                system, and your system should start responding to commands from the
                Bluetooth device.  Assume the Bluetooth device will come and go
                periodically, and make sure that your main loop does not depend on
                any particular state of the Bluetooth system.
            </p>
            <slide xml:id="sl-day17-ready">
    <ul>
        <li>Your system should behave normally even if there is no other device connected.</li>
        <li>When a Bluetooth device connects, it should start receiving data from your system, and your system should start responding to commands from the Bluetooth device.</li>
        <li>Assume the Bluetooth device will come and go periodically, and make sure that your main loop does not depend on any particular state of the Bluetooth system.</li>
    </ul>
    <note>≈ 1 min.  Her slide 22, "You are now ready to build your system!" as the headline.</note>
</slide>
        </subsection>

        <!-- Part 4: 7 min = 5 (project notes + paper design) + 2 (deadlines) -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day17-project">
            <title>Part 4: The Final Project, and Your Paper Design</title>
            <p>
                Read the project description carefully, and ask questions as needed.
                Approach the design as
                a series of small incremental steps: the deliverables are ordered
                to lead you to a functioning system fairly quickly.
            </p>
            <slide xml:id="sl-day17-project-notes">
    <ul>
        <li>Read the project description carefully.  Ask questions as needed.</li>
        <li>Get started with your paper design!  By Friday at 10 pm, submit your paper design (pseudocode, block diagram, and so on).</li>
        <li>Approach the design as a series of small incremental steps.  The deliverables are ordered to lead you to a functioning system fairly quickly.</li>
    </ul>
    <note>≈ 2 min.  Her slide 23, plus the lab's staging sentence.</note>
</slide>
            <activity xml:id="act-day17-paper">
                <title>Begin your paper design</title>
                <introduction>
                    <p>This is a large, complicated project.  Do not start coding
                    until you have developed a plan!</p>
                </introduction>
                <task xml:id="task-day17-diagram">
                    <statement><p>Begin the process on paper by describing, with a
                    block diagram, high-level pseudocode, or a state machine
                    diagram, exactly which components you are using and how they
                    will interact.  Consider in particular:</p>
                    <p><ul>
                        <li>Are you using timers, and if so, which ones?</li>
                        <li>Will you need interrupts, and which ones?</li>
                        <li>What components will you use (buttons, display, motors, and so on)?</li>
                        <li>If you are using a state machine, what are its states?</li>
                    </ul></p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day17-io">
                    <statement><p>Include a listing of all the input and output in
                    your system and, importantly, how you are using it.  Rather
                    than state that you are using a button, make notes detailing
                    how often you will sample it, or whether an interrupt reads
                    it, and how you will use the value to decide what happens
                    next.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day17-pseudo">
                    <statement><p>If you write pseudocode, keep it at a high level
                    and abstract; this will help you focus on the bigger picture
                    before you get mired in the details.  Do not write C!
                    Non-obvious steps, such as resetting a pin, should carry a
                    note about why you are doing them.</p></statement>
                </task>
            </activity>
            <p>
                Initially there will be a lot missing, but approaching the problem
                top-down at a high level allows you to evolve your design
                gradually, so that without too much effort you can map from your
                high-level ideas to the low-level pieces we have been studying all
                term.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <!-- Part 5: 49 min of project work time, then the close (1 min) -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day17-work">
            <title>Part 5: Project Work Time</title>
            <p>
                You will have the rest of the class to work on your project.
                Your paper design is due Friday at 10 pm, and the project demos are
                on Tuesday.
            </p>
        </subsection>

    </section>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <section xml:id="sec-ble-reference">
        <title>Reference: Talking to the Bluefruit</title>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Lookup material for the radio link, arranged for a quick look-up
                while you build.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-ble-ref-flow">
            <title>Hardware Flow Control</title>
            <p>
                Flow control is described in the
                <url href="external/stm32c031_rm.pdf">Reference Manual</url>,
                RM0490 §24.5.20, <em>RS232 Hardware flow control and RS485 Driver
                Enable</em>; the crossover between the two devices is drawn there
                as Figure 263.  RTS and CTS flow control are enabled independently
                by writing the RTSE and CTSE bits to 1 in the <c>USART_CR3</c>
                register.  Both bits can only be written while the USART is
                disabled (UE = 0 in <c>USART_CR1</c>); a write to
                <c>USART_CR3</c> after the USART has been enabled has no effect
                (Reference Manual, RM0490 §24.8.4).
            </p>
            <p>
                On the Bluefruit's side of the link, TXO is the module's transmit
                pin at 3.3 V logic, RXI is its receive pin, RTS is its
                request-to-send output, low when it is fine to send data to the
                module, and CTS is its clear-to-send input, pulled high by default
                and enabling data out of TXO only while it is held low.  The module
                runs at 9600 baud as shipped, and VIN is powered from 3.3 V.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-ble-ref-protocol-tab">
            <title>The AD2 as a UART: the Protocol Tab</title>
            <p>
                The Protocol tab is in the main Waveforms menu, on the left-hand
                side of the welcome tab.  In it, the AD2 acts as another UART device
                that can send and receive.  In the protocol settings you define
                which AD2 DIO pin acts as Tx and which acts as Rx, and you set the
                baud rate to match yours, 9600.  The AD2's Tx must be connected to
                the receiver of the USART under test, and its Rx to the
                transmitter.  Send one character at a time: the AD2 does not
                support hardware flow control, so nothing slows it down when your
                program falls behind.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-ble-ref-at">
            <title>The AT Commands, and the Module's Timing Rules</title>
            <p>
                In command mode the module accepts AT commands and replies OK or
                ERROR.  The full command set is described in the
                <url href="https://learn.adafruit.com/introducing-the-adafruit-bluefruit-le-uart-friend/command-mode-1">Adafruit
                documentation</url>; the three used in Lab 9 are these.
            </p>
            <ul>
                <li><c>ATZ</c> performs a soft reset, which restarts the Bluetooth stack.</li>
                <li><c>AT+FACTORYRESET</c> restores the module to a known state; in Lab 9 you are advised to issue it at startup so you know exactly what state your device is in.</li>
                <li><c>AT+GAPDEVNAME=chosenName</c> sets the name the module advertises.</li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                In Lab 9 you are given two timing rules to follow for a reliable
                startup routine.  After sending a command, wait a few milliseconds
                (<c>delay_ms()</c> is fine) and then process the response.  You do
                not necessarily need to do anything with it, but you should keep
                watching for available characters until the response is done, which
                you can tell from having seen no new character for 10 or 20
                milliseconds.  After extracting the response, wait an additional
                200 ms before sending another command.
            </p>
        </subsection>
    </section>
</chapter>
